WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.002
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book two.

2
00:00:05.302 --> 00:00:08.607
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.907 --> 00:00:15.100
Unit four: Cognitive Linguistics. Today you will reason about language using hypothetical inversion.

4
00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:18.064
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:22.070
Part one: words. You will learn ten new words.

6
00:00:22.917 --> 00:00:24.533
Conceptual metaphor.

7
00:00:25.517 --> 00:00:30.349
Conceptual metaphor means a mapping from one idea onto another, as in 'time is money'.

8
00:00:30.950 --> 00:00:34.064
'Argument is war' is a conceptual metaphor in English.

9
00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:37.790
Your turn. Say: Conceptual metaphor.

10
00:00:40.633 --> 00:00:42.296
Conceptual metaphor.

11
00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:44.376
Semantic field.

12
00:00:45.383 --> 00:00:48.544
Semantic field means a group of words related in meaning.

13
00:00:49.150 --> 00:00:52.055
'Doctor', 'nurse' and 'ward' belong to one semantic field.

14
00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:55.546
Your turn. Say: Semantic field.

15
00:00:58.400 --> 00:00:59.703
Semantic field.

16
00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:01.660
Pragmatics.

17
00:01:02.650 --> 00:01:07.099
Pragmatics means the study of how context affects meaning in communication.

18
00:01:07.683 --> 00:01:11.520
Pragmatics explains why 'Can you pass the salt?' is a request.

19
00:01:12.117 --> 00:01:14.502
Your turn. Say: Pragmatics.

20
00:01:17.367 --> 00:01:18.554
Pragmatics.

21
00:01:19.317 --> 00:01:20.643
Cognitive frame.

22
00:01:21.650 --> 00:01:25.937
Cognitive frame means a mental structure that shapes how we interpret events.

23
00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:29.803
The phrase 'tax relief' activates a cognitive frame of burden.

24
00:01:30.417 --> 00:01:33.278
Your turn. Say: Cognitive frame.

25
00:01:36.133 --> 00:01:37.506
Cognitive frame.

26
00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:39.994
Linguistic relativity.

27
00:01:40.983 --> 00:01:45.154
Linguistic relativity means the idea that language influences thought.

28
00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:50.455
Linguistic relativity is also known as the Sapir-Whorf hypothesis.

29
00:01:51.050 --> 00:01:54.202
Your turn. Say: Linguistic relativity.

30
00:01:57.067 --> 00:01:58.939
Linguistic relativity.

31
00:01:59.683 --> 00:02:01.021
Prototype theory.

32
00:02:02.017 --> 00:02:07.325
Prototype theory means the idea that categories are organised around typical examples.

33
00:02:07.917 --> 00:02:11.902
By prototype theory, a robin is a better 'bird' than a penguin.

34
00:02:12.517 --> 00:02:15.459
Your turn. Say: Prototype theory.

35
00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:19.731
Prototype theory.

36
00:02:20.483 --> 00:02:22.030
Embodied cognition.

37
00:02:23.017 --> 00:02:27.257
Embodied cognition means the theory that thinking is shaped by the body's experience.

38
00:02:27.850 --> 00:02:31.905
Embodied cognition explains why 'grasping' an idea feels natural.

39
00:02:32.517 --> 00:02:36.040
Your turn. Say: Embodied cognition.

40
00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:40.424
Embodied cognition.

41
00:02:41.183 --> 00:02:42.417
Discourse marker.

42
00:02:43.417 --> 00:02:47.854
Discourse marker means a word that organises talk, such as 'well' or 'anyway'.

43
00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:51.727
Overusing the discourse marker 'like' can distract listeners.

44
00:02:52.317 --> 00:02:55.352
Your turn. Say: Discourse marker.

45
00:02:58.200 --> 00:02:59.642
Discourse marker.

46
00:03:00.383 --> 00:03:01.442
Syntax.

47
00:03:02.450 --> 00:03:06.237
Syntax means the rules governing how words combine into sentences.

48
00:03:06.850 --> 00:03:09.511
Khmer and English differ greatly in syntax.

49
00:03:10.117 --> 00:03:12.757
Your turn. Say: Syntax.

50
00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:16.717
Syntax.

51
00:03:17.483 --> 00:03:18.484
Cognition.

52
00:03:19.483 --> 00:03:23.387
Cognition means the mental processes of knowing, thinking and perceiving.

53
00:03:23.983 --> 00:03:26.621
Language and cognition influence one another.

54
00:03:27.217 --> 00:03:29.753
Your turn. Say: Cognition.

55
00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:33.694
Cognition.

56
00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:38.453
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

57
00:03:39.317 --> 00:03:42.686
Visal, does the language we speak actually change how we think?

58
00:03:43.783 --> 00:03:51.695
That is the question of linguistic relativity. Were it fully true, language would constrain thought entirely.

59
00:03:52.783 --> 00:03:54.400
And is it fully true?

60
00:03:55.483 --> 00:04:05.763
Few believe so. Were speakers truly trapped by their vocabulary, translation would be impossible, yet it plainly happens every day.

61
00:04:06.850 --> 00:04:08.513
So the effect is partial?

62
00:04:09.617 --> 00:04:21.351
Exactly. Had researchers claimed total determinism, they would have been refuted long ago. Subtle influence on attention and memory is far more defensible.

63
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:24.035
Part three: grammar.

64
00:04:24.883 --> 00:04:28.137
Grammar focus: Hypothetical linguistic reasoning.

65
00:04:28.437 --> 00:04:29.589
Look at the pattern.

66
00:04:30.283 --> 00:04:34.280
Were linguistic relativity fully true, language would constrain thought.

67
00:04:35.183 --> 00:04:38.913
Had researchers claimed total determinism, they would have been refuted.

68
00:04:39.817 --> 00:04:43.476
Should the evidence prove robust, theories would need revising.

69
00:04:44.433 --> 00:04:53.936
Drop 'if' and invert: Were it true (= If it were true), Had she known (= If she had known), Should you need help (= If you should need help). It sounds formal and precise.

70
00:04:54.683 --> 00:04:59.066
Be careful! Don't say: Was language fully free, thought would be free.

71
00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:04.579
Say: Were language fully free, thought would be free.

72
00:05:04.879 --> 00:05:07.250
Inversion uses 'were' for all persons.

73
00:05:08.033 --> 00:05:10.918
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

74
00:05:11.733 --> 00:05:13.152
Language and Thought.

75
00:05:13.452 --> 00:05:20.015
Cognitive linguistics studies how language reflects, and perhaps shapes, the ways in which humans conceptualise the world.

76
00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:27.899
Lakoff and Johnson demonstrated that everyday speech is saturated with conceptual metaphor, such as the treatment of argument as war.

77
00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:35.509
Prototype theory suggests that categories are not defined by strict boundaries but organised around typical examples.

78
00:05:36.500 --> 00:05:44.653
Were linguistic relativity fully true, speakers of different languages would be unable to understand one another, yet translation succeeds daily.

79
00:05:45.667 --> 00:05:53.471
Most researchers therefore defend a weaker claim: that language subtly influences attention and memory without determining thought.

80
00:05:54.567 --> 00:05:58.696
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

81
00:05:59.550 --> 00:06:02.803
blank the theory fully true, language would constrain thought.

82
00:06:03.103 --> 00:06:06.072
A, Was. B, Were. C, Is.

83
00:06:09.633 --> 00:06:11.203
The answer is B: Were.

84
00:06:12.350 --> 00:06:15.789
A word that organises talk, such as 'anyway', is a blank.

85
00:06:16.089 --> 00:06:21.066
A, discourse marker. B, prototype. C, semantic field.

86
00:06:24.600 --> 00:06:27.157
The answer is A: discourse marker.

87
00:06:28.317 --> 00:06:31.082
The idea that language influences thought is blank.

88
00:06:31.382 --> 00:06:36.243
A, syntax. B, embodied cognition. C, linguistic relativity.

89
00:06:39.800 --> 00:06:42.798
The answer is C: linguistic relativity.

90
00:06:43.950 --> 00:06:45.833
Choose the correct inversion.

91
00:06:46.133 --> 00:06:53.699
A, Had researchers known, they would have acted.. B, Had researchers knew, they would acted.. C, Have researchers known, they would act..

92
00:06:57.233 --> 00:07:00.719
The answer is A: Had researchers known, they would have acted..

93
00:07:01.883 --> 00:07:04.568
According to the reading, most researchers defend…

94
00:07:04.868 --> 00:07:09.833
A, total determinism. B, a weaker claim. C, no effect at all.

95
00:07:13.367 --> 00:07:15.749
The answer is B: a weaker claim.

96
00:07:16.933 --> 00:07:20.166
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

97
00:07:21.017 --> 00:07:23.330
How might language shape the way people think?

98
00:07:40.117 --> 00:07:41.675
Now listen to Visal.

99
00:07:41.975 --> 00:08:12.685
Language probably shapes thought in subtle ways, mainly by directing attention. Were it the whole story, translation would be impossible, so I reject the strongest version. However, conceptual metaphors such as 'time is money' clearly influence how we treat time. Words also create cognitive frames: calling a policy a 'burden' or an 'investment' changes our reaction. Language therefore guides thinking without imprisoning it.

100
00:08:12.985 --> 00:08:14.752
Excellent answer, Visal!

101
00:08:15.650 --> 00:08:20.508
Great work! Now you can use ten new words and hypothetical linguistic reasoning.

102
00:08:20.808 --> 00:08:26.142
Today's value is linguistic awareness. Notice how the words you choose shape the way you think.

103
00:08:26.442 --> 00:08:28.967
See you in Unit five. Goodbye!

104
00:08:29.267 --> 00:08:32.790
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

